    detail: >-
      Five finders, eleven candidates, three confirmed. (1) ALTITUDE, important: the new UC-4 block in
      docs/use-cases/web-ui.html describes design run 11 as "archived", but the run is still tracked in
      docs/work/ at the tip and docs/.archive/ is empty - DEC-28 says a run stays in docs/work/ until the
      epic's cleanup, so archival is an epic-close event and the claim is premature. The verifier found the
      precedent that settles it: commit d475bef introduced this exact plain-text "archived" idiom
      specifically to REPLACE a live href once E-REDESIGN's run was actually git rm'd at its epic close, so
      the idiom is tied to git-tier removal. The pre-existing line 79 uses it correctly (its target really
      is absent from git); the new line copies the idiom onto a file that is still live. Mitigating and
      recorded: run 11 does carry data-status="archived" internally, which is a different, gate-checked
      vocabulary meaning the run is closed to iteration - true, but invisible in rendered prose and not the
      sense the idiom carries in T2. (2) CORRECTNESS, important: openFile and its browser fallback
      pickThroughInput (ui/src/body.ts) shipped with no unit test - body.test.ts is untouched by the diff,
      and every test that exercises "choosing a file" mocks openFile itself, so neither the Tauri branch nor
      the cancel/change listeners ever run. The verifier established this is an omission and not a harness
      limit: jsdom dispatches synthetic cancel/change events against a captured input, which is the standard
      technique. Against CLAUDE.md's "no change lands without its test" at commit level, with no waiver.
      (3) CORRECTNESS, nit: the export and import flows share one `transfer` state, and the import item is
      never disabled during an export, so opening the import dialog mid-export and merely DISMISSING it
      calls onBusy(false) -> setTransfer(null), clearing the export's "exporting..." line while its promise
      is still pending. Reproduced live in a throwaway test. The finder's stronger claims - a second
      concurrent exportHar, and the two flows clobbering each other's failure state - were refuted: the
      dialog is a true modal whose z-modal backdrop covers the z-popup menu and whose Tab trap excludes it,
      so the trigger is unreachable while it is open, and the two `failure` values are separate local state.
      Eight refuted. REUSE x2: CaptureMenu duplicating Select's popup mechanics and its panel className -
      design run 11 foresees that duplication verbatim ("the owner should decide whether Menu duplicates
      that shell or the shell is extracted and both consume it. This run has one sighting of a menu and is
      not entitled to pick") and its Proposals section authorises W6 to build local and forbids it from
      minting, so the finding asks the build to make a call the design reserved for someone else; the
      className overlap is the same condition restated, and ui/eslint-rules/vocabulary.js validates each
      design-allow pragma per occurrence, so a second copy cannot silently drift. REUSE: reasonOf
      duplicating Onboarding's error-unwrap - two sites of a one-line idiom, one pre-existing, different
      contracts (a server-authored refusal vs a generic catch), under the rubric's "inventing new
      abstractions to force sharing" exclusion. CORRECTNESS: dismissing a dialog mid-flight setting state
      after unmount - React 18.3.1 makes that a documented silent no-op, the unguarded dismissal is the
      pre-existing ProxyCloseDialog pattern this diff EXTRACTED rather than introduced, and run 11 treats a
      dismissal as deliberately silent. SIMPLIFICATION: ImportDialog's local `importing` beside the parent's
      `transfer` - the proposed remedy is unworkable, since onBusy is the only channel by which the parent
      learns an import started; the verifier also noted the success path never clears `importing`, which is
      moot because the component unmounts. SIMPLIFICATION: an unread ChosenFile.name - saveBody returns a
      name callers already discard, the symmetry is documented in the file, and run 11 never asks for the
      chosen filename to be shown. SIMPLIFICATION: the computed CAPTURE_COPY[`${transfer}ing`] lookup - the
      verifier proved with tsc that renaming the key fails the build at that exact line, so it is
      typo-proof, and the residual complaint is grep convenience with no principle behind it. EFFICIENCY:
      the Blob copy in saveBody's non-Tauri branch - unreachable in the shipped Tauri app, bounded by the
      ring's 256 MB cap, and in code this diff does not touch.
